[AIMessage] **Ring of Nestor**

The Ring of Nestor is the modern name given to a Late Bronze Age gold signet ring found in the Griffin Warrior Tomb near
Pylos, in Messenia, Greece. The tomb was excavated by a University of Cincinnati-led team, and the ring is generally 
dated to around the 16th century BCE. It is discussed as part of the tomb’s elite grave goods and for its unusually 
detailed miniature engraving. [1][2]

The bezel of the ring shows a ritual scene in Minoan style, with a seated female figure, a tree or shrine, and 
additional figures in a landscape setting. The ring’s workmanship and iconography have been compared with seal engraving
from Bronze Age Crete, suggesting close artistic contact between Crete and the mainland Greek world. [2][3]

The nickname “Ring of Nestor” refers to Nestor, the Homeric ruler of Pylos, and reflects the ring’s findspot rather than
any direct connection with the epic character. The name has been used in both popular and scholarly discussion of the 
find because the tomb was discovered near the Bronze Age center later associated with Nestor. [1][4]

Interpretations of the image differ in detail, but scholars commonly treat the ring as evidence for religious 
iconography and for the movement of Minoan-style artistic forms into Mycenaean burial contexts. The ring is therefore 
often cited in discussions of Mycenaean religion, elite identity, and cross-cultural exchange in the Aegean Bronze Age. 
[2][3]

**References**

[1] https://www.uc.edu/news/articles/2017/05/nr-ancient-gold-ring-found-in-griffin-warrior-tomb.html

[2] 
https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/160601-ancient-mycenaean-ring-greece-griffin-warrior-tomb-archaeology

[3] https://www.archaeology.org/issues/307-1709/trenches/5810-greece-griffin-warrior-tomb

[4] https://www.britannica.com/topic/Nestor-Greek-mythology
